  "synthesis_conclusions": [
    "There is a strong preference for daylight in workplaces, linked to beliefs about better health.",
    "People tend to overestimate daylight's contribution to overall illumination, with overestimation increasing with distance from windows.",
    "Preferred window size varies, but generally larger windows are preferred, with optimal office window sizes between 1.8-2.4m in height and wider than tall for a lateral view.",
    "Manually operated shading devices are typically set and then rarely changed.",
    "Preferred illuminance levels in daylit offices are highly variable among individuals, and desired additional electric light varies by task and distance from the window.",
    "Discomfort glare from windows is less problematic than predicted by glare index models, but is highly variable and depends on view quality, distance, and task.",
    "Photocontrolled lighting systems are best accepted with individual override control; fully automated systems have low acceptance and can be too complex to maintain.",
    "Photocontrolled shading devices also require overriding occupant controls for acceptance.",
    "Integrated lighting and shading controls are most accepted when manual control is provided.",
    "Control systems are more acceptable to occupants and facility managers when they are simple and easy to use."
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "Systematic study of luminous conditions created by manual lighting and shading controls to inform automated control algorithms.",
    "Systematic comparisons across various orientations, weather conditions, times of day, latitudes, seasons, building/window types, cultures, and individuals to establish generality of recommendations.",
    "Expansion of luminous conditions studied beyond simple horizontal illuminance to include vertical luminances, luminance ratios, and other metrics.",
    "Study of the relationship between discomfort glare, window view, overall luminous satisfaction, and chosen luminous conditions in relation to outdoor conditions to assess trade-offs.",
    "Comparison of automated systems using behaviourally-derived algorithms to semi-automated systems (with manual override) to determine incremental benefit.",
    "Comparison of luminous conditions chosen by individuals to their reported preferences to identify targets for training people to use controls.",
    "Analysis of various control interfaces and locations (wall-mounted, remote control, computer screen) in laboratory settings.",
    "Analysis of energy use from manual controls versus automated/no controls using laboratory and field data.",
    "Study of potential for conflict in open-plan spaces with manual/semi-automatic lighting and shading controls to identify system designs that minimize conflict while maintaining energy savings and acceptable lighting conditions."
  ],
